  "vulnerabilities": [
    {
      "type": "Fee-on-transfer / deflationary token share inflation leading to vault insolvency",
      "severity": "high",
      "vulnerable_function": "_deposit (used by deposit() and mint())",
      "location": "WERC7575Vault._deposit",
      "explanation": "The vault mints shares based on the user-supplied `assets` amount, but it does not verify how many tokens were actually received by the vault after `safeTransferFrom`. If the configured `_asset` is fee-on-transfer/deflationary (or otherwise transfers less than requested), the vault will mint shares as if the full `assets` amount arrived, while the vault balance increases by a smaller amount. This creates unbacked shares that can later be redeemed/withdrawn for full assets, draining funds deposited by honest users and/or making the vault insolvent.",
      "attack_scenario": "Assume `_asset` charges a 10% transfer fee.\n1) Honest users deposit 1000 tokens total; vault balance becomes 900 tokens but share supply reflects 1000 tokens worth of shares.\n2) Attacker deposits 100 tokens via `deposit(100, attacker)`; vault receives only 90 tokens but attacker receives shares representing 100 tokens.\n3) Attacker calls `redeem(sharesFor100, attacker, attacker)` (or withdraw equivalent). The vault attempts to pay assets corresponding to the inflated shares. If the vault has enough balance from other users, attacker withdraws more value than they contributed, effectively extracting the missing 10% from the pool.\n4) Repeating this drains the vault / causes later withdrawals to revert due to insufficient balance.",
      "suggested_fix": "Mint shares based on the actual amount received:\n- In `_deposit`, record `uint256 balBefore = IERC20Metadata(_asset).balanceOf(address(this));`\n- Perform the transferFrom\n- Compute `uint256 received = balanceAfter - balBefore;`\n- Compute shares from `received` (or, for `mint`, recompute received and enforce `received >= assetsRequired` with a revert).\nAdditionally, explicitly disallow fee-on-transfer/rebasing assets if the design expects stable, non-deflationary tokens (document and enforce via allowlist or invariant checks)."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "Core ERC4626-style flow is generally straightforward (nonReentrant on entrypoints, basic input checks, deterministic conversion). The main exploitable issue is that deposits/mints assume the transferred `assets` amount equals what the vault receives. With fee-on-transfer/deflationary tokens, this mints excess shares and enables value extraction from other depositors and/or insolvency. If the protocol strictly deploys only for standard non-fee stablecoins, this risk is mitigated operationally, but it is not enforced by the code."
}
